            <ArticleTitle>Aggressive differentiated thyroid cancer.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0748-7983(17)30709-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ejso.2017.09.019</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Differentiated thyroid cancer is characteristically associated with an innocuous clinical course, but a minority of cases may manifest surprisingly aggressive behaviour. Such aggressive DTC are directly responsible for the majority of thyroid cancer related deaths. Moreover, they contribute indirectly to increased DTC-related morbidity, because our inability to differentiate these tumours from innocuous DTC at an early stagefuels a significant degree of DTC overtreatment around the globe. In the present paper we describe how improved understanding of the clinicopathological thyroid tumour progression model and optimization of clinical staging systems continues to improve our ability to diagnose and treat aggressive DTC. Early recognition of aggressive DTC allows instillation of an aggressive management strategy which is based upon surgical-oncologic completeness, and minimization of treatment-related sequelae through continued development of reconstructive options and focussed delivery of adjuvant treatments.</AbstractText>
